(* Quest Self-Hosted Compiler: The Simplifier Interface
 *
 * The Core IR optimized into the simplified stage (docs/ir.md section 8.6),
 * before specialization and closure conversion, while functions are still
 * funs statements with free variables. The self-hosted compiler's alone: the
 * bootstrap has no such stage.
 *
 * The simplifier works in rounds, by default at most 6, until one changes
 * nothing. Each round propagates copies of variables and constants, folds
 * constants (an if or a switch on a constant becomes its branch), removes
 * the lets and funs nothing reads when they cannot raise, replaces a switch
 * on an option or variant made in scope by its case, and the tag tests,
 * payloads, and projections of the options, variants, and tuples (but their
 * var components) made in scope by what made them, and inlines calls
 * (questlang/ir/inline): a function's body replaces a call of it when the
 * function is small or called once, within a bound on the unit's growth.
 * Then contification (questlang/ir/contify) turns local functions called
 * from one place, and otherwise only by their own tail calls, into loops at
 * that place. Each round simplifies what the last one inlined and made
 * simpler. The environment variable QUEST_INLINE overrides the bounds and
 * thresholds, for measuring them (docs/inlining.md section 1). *)

interface Simplify
import
    questlang/ir/nodes : questlang/ir/Nodes
export
    (* Simplifies the unit's bodies, in place, returning it *)
    simplifyUnit(unit: nodes.Unit): nodes.Unit
    (* Whether evaluating an operation can do nothing but compute its value:
     * no effect, no exception *)
    pure(o: nodes.Op): Bool
end
